i['strategy']=r'''The Mackey support lemma [[lem-mackey-support-for-homs-between-finite-principal-series]] gives the Hom dimension as the number of Weyl elements carrying the target character to the source. If the characters lie in one Weyl orbit this number is exactly $N=|W_\chi|=|W_{\chi'}|$, giving (1) and (2). To prove (3), use [[thm-character-inner-product-computes-intertwiner-dimension]]: the characters $c,c'$ of the two modules have $\langle c,c\rangle=\langle c',c'\rangle=\langle c,c'\rangle=\langle c',c\rangle=N$, so $\langle c-c',c-c'\rangle=0$. Positivity of the finite class-function inner product gives $c=c'$. By Maschke and Schur, the multiplicity of every simple $V$ is $\langle c,\chi_V\rangle$, since each finite-dimensional complex division endomorphism ring is $\mathbb C$ (an endomorphism has an eigenvalue and a nonzero noninvertible difference from a scalar must vanish). Thus the two semisimple modules have the same finite simple decomposition and are isomorphic. This argument uses no conjugation of the fixed Borel and no arbitrary choice.'''
