i=items[C];i['statement']=i['statement'].replace('$ge_\\chi\\mapsto(g\\mapsto\\text{appropriate }B\\text{-covariant function})$',r'$ge_\chi\mapsto f_g$, where $f_g(gb)=\widetilde\chi(b)^{-1}$ and $f_g=0$ outside $gB$')
i['strategy']=r'''The covariance functions $f_g$ satisfy $f_{gb}=\widetilde\chi(b)f_g$, exactly the relation $gbe_\chi=\widetilde\chi(b)ge_\chi$; both models have the basis indexed by a finite left transversal, proving (1). Right multiplication $R_a$ satisfies $R_aR_b=R_{ba}$, giving the stated opposite-algebra identification. Bruhat decomposition and $e_\chi b=be_\chi=\widetilde\chi(b)e_\chi$ show spanning by $e_\chi\dot we_\chi$. For $t\in T$, the identities $e_\chi t\dot we_\chi=\chi(t)e_\chi\dot we_\chi=\chi(\dot w^{-1}t\dot w)e_\chi\dot we_\chi$ force vanishing unless $w\cdot\chi=\chi$. At most $|W_\chi|$ spanning vectors remain, and the dimension theorem [[thm-weyl-stabilizer-controls-principal-series-endomorphisms]] gives exactly that dimension, so all these vectors are nonzero and independent.'''
edit(B,statement=r'''For $w\in W_\chi$ use the canonical permutation matrix $\dot w$ and the ambient inversion length $\ell(w)$, and put $\Theta_w=q^{\ell(w)}e_\chi\dot we_\chi$ in the corner of [[lem-principal-series-endomorphisms-as-the-chi-idempotent-corner]]. Define $B_w:=R_{\Theta_{w^{-1}}}$ on $\mathbb C[G]e_\chi\cong I(\chi)$. The inverse index accounts for the opposite-algebra identification; $B_1=\mathrm{id}$ and these operators form a basis. Representative independence requires the character compensation: if $n_w=t\dot w$ with $t\in T$, use $\chi(t)^{-1}e_\chi n_we_\chi=e_\chi\dot we_\chi$. More generally replacing $\dot w$ by $b_1\dot wb_2$ requires division by $\widetilde\chi(b_1)\widetilde\chi(b_2)$; this factor is independent of the decomposition because $w$ stabilizes $\chi$ on $B\cap\dot wB\dot w^{-1}$. Thus the compensated operator is independent of representatives; the uncompensated corner element generally is not. For $\chi=1$ this is the usual spherical basis with inverse index in the endomorphism model. Raw $B_w$ for unsorted characters use ambient length; a Hecke basis after Weyl sorting is specified later.''',strategy=r'''Apply the corner basis lemma and right multiplication with inverse index. A torus factor multiplies the corner element by its character value, so compensate it rather than pretending it disappears. For a general double-coset representative, character covariance gives the displayed factor; two decompositions differ by an element of the Borel intersection, where the two inflated characters agree. This is the one-dimensional version of the simultaneous representative and $\gamma_n$ convention in Dudas--Michel §11.3. These are finite sums and use no choice principle.''')
edit(LB,statement=r'''The canonical, compensated operators $B_w$ defined in [[def-standard-intertwining-operators-for-finite-principal-series]], $w\in W_\chi$, form a $\mathbb C$-basis of $\operatorname{End}_G I(\chi)$. Their dimension is $|W_\chi|$. For two characters, the analogous corner between their idempotents, with the covariance compensation and the opposite orientation matched to its source and target, gives a Hom basis indexed by $\{w:\chi=w\cdot\chi'\}$.''',strategy=r'''The corner lemma [[lem-principal-series-endomorphisms-as-the-chi-idempotent-corner]] proves the basis: inverse indexing permutes $W_\chi$ and right multiplication is a vector-space isomorphism. For Hom spaces, Bruhat decomposition gives spanning by the corresponding mixed-corner elements, torus covariance kills precisely the unsupported indices, and [[lem-mackey-support-for-homs-between-finite-principal-series]] supplies exactly the number of remaining indices. Hence these elements are nonzero and independent. Independence of representatives means the compensated convention, not independence of an uncompensated $e_\chi n_we_\chi$.''')
edit(LP,statement=r'''For Weyl-sorted $\eta=(a_1^{n_1},\ldots,a_k^{n_k})$ with distinct $a_r$, put $L=\prod_r\operatorname{GL}_{n_r}(\mathbb F_q)$ and $\rho(l)=\prod_r a_r(\det l_r)$. With the canonical intertwiners $B_w$ of [[def-standard-intertwining-operators-for-finite-principal-series]], set $T_w=\rho(\dot w)^{-1}B_w$ for $w\in W_\eta$. Then $T_uT_v=T_{uv}$ whenever the intrinsic (equivalently here ambient) lengths add, and the simple $T_s$ satisfy all type-A braid and commuting relations. The raw basis also has length-additive products $B_uB_v=B_{uv}$ in these sorted coordinates. For arbitrary $\chi$, choose the prescribed sorting $\eta$ and a module isomorphism $J:I(\chi)\to I(\eta)$ from [[thm-weyl-stabilizer-controls-principal-series-endomorphisms]]; transport the normalized basis through $J$. Its labels and length are transported through the sorting permutation. This does not identify the unsorted raw ambient-length basis with the transported basis.''',strategy=r'''The principal series in $L$ is the tensor product of spherical principal series in its factors twisted by $\rho$, since $\rho|_{B\cap L}=\eta$. The algebra automorphism $g\mapsto\rho(g)g$ sends $e_\eta$ to $e_{B\cap L}$ and $q^{\ell(w)}e_\eta\dot we_\eta$ to $\rho(\dot w)q^{\ell(w)}e_{B\cap L}\dot we_{B\cap L}$. Right multiplication with inverse labels transfers the spherical length-additive products and braid relations to the $T_w$. Here $\rho(\dot w)=\prod_r a_r(-1)^{\ell(w_r)}\in\{1,-1\}$ and is a group character, so it also transfers the length-additive products to $B_w$. Harish--Chandra induction from $L$ to $G$ preserves these operator identities and identifies $R_L^G I_L(\eta)$ with $I_G(\eta)$ by [[thm-transitivity-and-parabolic-independence-of-harish-chandra-induction]]. The induced operators are exactly the displayed $B_w$: writing $e_\eta^G=e_{U_P}e_\eta^L$ and observing that $L$ normalizes $U_P$ gives $e_\eta^G\dot we_\eta^G=e_{U_P}e_\eta^L\dot we_\eta^L$. Finally conjugating all operators by $J$ preserves every product. There is no inference of braid relations merely from the corresponding permutation identities.''',deps=[LB,W,D,B,'lem-length-increasing-hecke-products','thm-type-a-iwahori-hecke-presentation','thm-transitivity-and-parabolic-independence-of-harish-chandra-induction','def-harish-chandra-induction-and-restriction-for-finite-gl-n','thm-levi-decomposition-of-standard-parabolics-in-gl-n-fq'])
edit(RP,statement=r'''For Weyl-sorted $\eta$, each simple $s\in S_\eta$ exchanges two adjacent equal coordinates $a,a$. The associated rank-one Levi has principal series $(a\circ\det)\oplus(\operatorname{St}\otimes a\circ\det)$ on its $\operatorname{GL}_2$ factor, tensored with one-dimensional characters on the remaining torus factors, with constituent degrees $1,q$. The canonical raw intertwiner has eigenvalues $a(-1)q$ and $-a(-1)$. The normalized $T_s=a(-1)^{-1}B_s$ satisfies $T_s^2=(q-1)T_s+q\,\mathrm{id}$; thus the parameter is exactly $q$, and the normalization of [[lem-length-additive-products-of-standard-intertwiners]] gives $\lambda_w=\prod_r a_r(-1)^{-\ell(w_r)}$. For arbitrary $\chi$ this assertion holds for the transported generators after Weyl sorting. In particular the naive generator fails the parameter-$q$ relation when $a(-1)=-1$: for $q=3$ and the nontrivial character of $\mathbb F_3^\times$, its eigenvalue on $a\circ\det$ is $-3$, which is neither $3$ nor $-1$.''',strategy=r'''Use the determinant twist in [[lem-length-additive-products-of-standard-intertwiners]] on the rank-one Levi. The twist sends the raw corner generator to $a(\det\dot s)T_s^{\mathrm{sph}}=a(-1)T_s^{\mathrm{sph}}$, and the spherical quadratic relation gives exactly the eigenvalues $a(-1)q,-a(-1)$ and their rescaling. The equal-character splitting and degrees are [[lem-equal-coordinate-rank-one-principal-series-of-gl2-fq]]. Harish--Chandra induction preserves the quadratic polynomial identity, and sorting transports it to all characters. This explicitly computes the normalization and does not use an unread formula for the quotient of constituent degrees; Dudas--Michel Theorem 11.11 provides an independent structural cross-check only.''',deps=['lem-equal-coordinate-rank-one-principal-series-of-gl2-fq',LP,B,D,'lem-rank-one-hecke-quadratic-relation'])
edit(G,statement=r'''Assume the Axiom of Choice, used for the Tits-deformation conclusion below. Let $\chi$ be any torus character of $G=\operatorname{GL}_n(\mathbb F_q)$, with equal-character block sizes $n_1,\ldots,n_k$ and stabilizer $W_\chi\cong\prod_rS_{n_r}$. Then $\operatorname{End}_G I(\chi)\cong\bigotimes_r H_q(S_{n_r})=H_q(W_\chi)$, with every parameter exactly $q$. In Weyl-sorted coordinates $\eta=(a_1^{n_1},\ldots,a_k^{n_k})$ the Hecke basis maps to $T_w=\lambda_wB_w$, where $\lambda_w=\prod_r a_r(-1)^{-\ell(w_r)}$ and $B_w$ uses right multiplication with inverse indexing. For an unsorted character the Hecke basis is transported from this sorted module through a module isomorphism; this statement makes no equality claim between that transported basis and the raw ambient-length Bruhat basis. Consequently $\operatorname{End}_G I(\chi)$ is semisimple and is abstractly isomorphic to $\mathbb C[W_\chi]$, preserving simple-module dimensions. Characters in the same $S_n$-orbit give isomorphic principal-series modules and endomorphism algebras. The endomorphism algebra identification with $H_q(W_\chi)$ itself uses no choice principle.''',strategy=r'''First sort the coordinates as in [[def-diagonal-torus-characters-and-weyl-action]], using the Weyl-orbit module isomorphism [[thm-weyl-stabilizer-controls-principal-series-endomorphisms]]. For sorted $\eta$, let $L=\prod_r\operatorname{GL}_{n_r}$, $P=L\ltimes U_P$ and $M=I_L(\eta)$. Transitivity identifies $R_L^GM=I_G(\eta)$. Inflation and induction give a unital algebra homomorphism $\operatorname{End}_L M\to\operatorname{End}_G R_L^GM$. It is injective: if $f(v)\ne0$, the induced function supported on $P$ with value $v$ at $1$ is sent to one whose value at $1$ is $f(v)\ne0$. Determinant twisting identifies $\operatorname{End}_L M$ with the tensor product of the spherical endomorphism algebras; [[thm-type-a-iwahori-hecke-presentation]] identifies these with $H_q(S_{n_r})$, using inverse indexing to remove the opposite. Its dimension is $\prod_r n_r!$ by the standard basis. The Mackey dimension theorem gives the same dimension for $\operatorname{End}_G I_G(\eta)$, so the injection is onto. The exact normalized basis and all relations follow from [[lem-length-additive-products-of-standard-intertwiners]] and [[lem-rank-one-hecke-parameter-for-equal-torus-characters]], including the explicit $\lambda_w$. Conjugating by the sorting module isomorphism proves the full arbitrary-character assertion. Finally [[thm-tits-deformation-for-the-type-a-hecke-algebra]], under AC, identifies each factor with $\mathbb C[S_{n_r}]$; tensoring gives $\mathbb C[W_\chi]$. Semisimplicity also follows directly from Maschke and the finite semisimple-module endomorphism lemma. This is a local proof of the split general-linear specialization corroborated by Dudas--Michel Theorem 11.11, rather than an application of Taylor's spherical result to a nontrivial character without the twist and faithful induction argument.''',deps=[W,LB,LP,RP,T,D,B,'def-generic-type-a-hecke-algebra','thm-standard-basis-of-the-generic-type-a-hecke-algebra','thm-type-a-iwahori-hecke-presentation','thm-transitivity-and-parabolic-independence-of-harish-chandra-induction','def-harish-chandra-induction-and-restriction-for-finite-gl-n','lem-constituent-multiplicities-under-a-semisimple-endomorphism-algebra','def-axiom-of-choice'])
# Close idempotent lifting: compressed lifts are approximate idempotents, not idempotents until re-lifted.
i=items['lem-lifting-idempotents-in-complete-deformation-algebras'];i['statement']=i['statement'].replace('orthogonal families','finite orthogonal families');i['strategy']=r'''Put $y=2x-1$ and $\varepsilon=y^2-1\in IA$. The binomial series $f(\varepsilon)=\sum_{m\ge0}\binom{-1/2}{m}\varepsilon^m$ converges because $\varepsilon^m\in I^mA$ and the filtration is complete and separated. Multiplication is continuous; the formal scalar identity $(1+z)f(z)^2=1$ therefore gives $(yf(\varepsilon))^2=1$, since $y$ commutes with $\varepsilon$ and its series. Hence $e=(1+yf(\varepsilon))/2$ is an idempotent congruent to $x$. For finitely many orthogonal quotient idempotents, lift the first and work next in $(1-e)A(1-e)$. This corner is a complete separated direct summand (the continuous idempotent projection is $a\mapsto(1-e)a(1-e)$). Compressed representatives are only approximate idempotents; apply the same series there to obtain genuine idempotents. Iterate finitely. If the quotient family sums to $1$, take the final lift to be the complement of the preceding lifts. No countable choice or Complete Nakayama is used.'''
# Fill exact radical interface in trace supplier.
