# Local formal supplier before Tits.
f={'id':F,'kind':'lemma','title':'Formal triviality of a semisimple one-parameter deformation','deps':['lem-lifting-idempotents-in-complete-deformation-algebras','lem-trace-form-nondegeneracy-characterizes-semisimple-finite-dimensional-algebras','prop-units-in-an-adically-complete-ring','def-adic-completion-of-a-module'],'statement':r'''Let $R=\mathbb C[[t]]$ and let $A$ be an associative unital $R$-algebra free of finite rank. If $A/tA\cong\prod_iM_{d_i}(\mathbb C)$, then $A\cong\prod_iM_{d_i}(R)$ as $R$-algebras. Every finite-rank $R$-linear map that is an isomorphism modulo $t$ is an isomorphism, by the determinant unit criterion. No choice principle is assumed.''','strategy':r'''Lift one diagonal primitive idempotent $e_i$ from each matrix block by [[lem-lifting-idempotents-in-complete-deformation-algebras]], and put $V_i=Ae_i$. Right multiplication by $e_i$ is an idempotent $R$-linear projection on the free module $A$, with image $V_i$ and kernel $A(1-e_i)$. Lift a finite basis of each of these two summands modulo $t$ into that summand. Their union has a square coordinate matrix invertible modulo $t$, so its determinant has nonzero constant term and is a unit, by [[prop-units-in-an-adically-complete-ring]] after dividing by that constant term. Its adjugate is an inverse, so the union is a basis of $A$. The projection is diagonal on this basis, proving $V_i$ is free of rank $d_i$ and reduces to the natural module of the $i$th matrix block. The left action gives $A\to\prod_i\operatorname{End}_R(V_i)$; modulo $t$ this is the faithful sum of natural representations, an isomorphism $\prod_iM_{d_i}(\mathbb C)\to\prod_iM_{d_i}(\mathbb C)$. Source and target are free of the same rank $\sum_i d_i^2$. Again the determinant unit criterion makes the action map an isomorphism. This explicitly supplies the direct-summand freeness and residue-isomorphism steps suppressed in Losev Theorem 2.6, Step 5. No Complete Nakayama, projective-module theorem or dependent choice is invoked.''','provenance':{'statement':'literature-derived','proof':'ai-altered'},'sources':{'references':[{'title':'Ivan Losev, Lecture 8, Theorem 2.6, Steps 4-5, PDF p. 5','url':'https://ivanloseu.github.io/RT/RT8.pdf','locator':'Theorem 2.6, Steps 4-5, PDF p. 5'}]},'dependency_level':1}
p[0]['items'].insert(p[0]['items'].index(items[T]),f);items[F]=f
edit(T,statement=r'''Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $X=D(f)\subseteq\mathbb A^1_{\mathbb C}$ be a nonempty principal open, let $A$ be a unital associative $\mathbb C[z,1/f]$-algebra free of finite rank, and let $x,y\in X$ have semisimple fibers. Then $A_x\cong A_y$ as $\mathbb C$-algebras. In particular, for every prime power $q$, $H_q(S_n)\cong e_B\mathbb C[\operatorname{GL}_n(\mathbb F_q)]e_B\cong\mathbb C[S_n]$, preserving the number and dimensions of simple modules. AC is used through the published Chevalley constructibility and strong Nullstellensatz suppliers, not in the formal lifting or determinant argument.''',strategy=r'''Fix a free basis and write its multiplication coefficients as rational functions in $z$ with denominators powers of $f$. The trace-form determinant $\Delta(z)$ is regular; [[lem-trace-form-nondegeneracy-characterizes-semisimple-finite-dimensional-algebras]] makes $Y=D(f\Delta)$ exactly the semisimple locus, containing $x,y$. This is a nonempty principal open of the line. At $x$, the homomorphism $\mathbb C[z,1/(f\Delta)]\to R=\mathbb C[[t]]$ sends $z$ to $x+t$; the denominator is a unit because its constant term is nonzero. The completion is explicitly $R$: modulo $(z-x)^m$, localization of the denominator does nothing because it is already a unit, so the inverse limit is the compatible truncated power-series ring. Base change gives a free complete algebra reducing to $A_x$. By [[lem-formal-triviality-of-one-parameter-semisimple-algebras]] it is isomorphic to $R\otimes_\mathbb C A_x$, providing a matrix $P(t)$ with unit determinant satisfying all multiplication-preservation equations. To pass from formal solutions to classical fibers, define the affine incidence algebraic set $Z_x$ of $(z,P,h,u)$ by those equations after clearing denominators and by $h\det P=1$ and $u(f\Delta)(z)=1$. The projection to $Y$ is a classical morphism by [[def-classical-affine-variety-morphism]]; its image $E_x$ is exactly the locus of fibers isomorphic to $A_x$. By [[thm-chevalley-constructible-image-varieties]] it is constructible (this supplier explicitly assumes AC). If $E_x$ were finite, the nonzero polynomial $g(z)=\prod_{a\in E_x}(z-a)$ would vanish on every complex point of $Z_x$. [[thm-affine-nullstellensatz-correspondence]], specifically $I(V(J))=\sqrt J$ (under AC), would give $g^N$ in the ideal defining $Z_x$. But the formal isomorphism, together with $h(t)=(\det P(t))^{-1}$ and $u(t)=(f\Delta)(x+t)^{-1}$, is a solution of these equations in $\mathbb C[[t]]$. Substitution would imply $g(x+t)^N=0$, impossible in the power-series domain for a nonzero polynomial. Thus $E_x$ is infinite. A constructible subset of a principal open of the line is finite or cofinite: every proper closed subset is finite, so in a finite union of locally closed pieces an infinite piece must contain a nonempty open, whose complement is finite. Hence $E_x$ is cofinite in $Y$. Repeat at $y$; the two cofinite sets meet because $Y$ is infinite. A fiber at a point of their intersection is isomorphic to both $A_x,A_y$, proving the theorem. This fills Losev's formal-to-Zariski step without claiming that an object-only coordinate-duality theorem proves morphism facts, or that formal triviality alone proves an orbit preimage open. Finally apply this to the free standard-basis algebra $\mathbb C[v^{\pm1}]\otimes H_v(n)$ at $1,q$, whose fibers are semisimple by [[prop-group-algebra-and-finite-field-specializations-of-the-generic-hecke-algebra]] and [[lem-semisimplicity-and-trace-form-for-the-finite-spherical-hecke-algebra]].''',deps=['thm-standard-basis-of-the-generic-type-a-hecke-algebra','prop-group-algebra-and-finite-field-specializations-of-the-generic-hecke-algebra','lem-semisimplicity-and-trace-form-for-the-finite-spherical-hecke-algebra','lem-trace-form-nondegeneracy-characterizes-semisimple-finite-dimensional-algebras',F,'def-principal-open-classical-variety','def-classical-affine-variety-morphism','thm-chevalley-constructible-image-varieties','thm-affine-nullstellensatz-correspondence','def-axiom-of-choice'])
